1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,280
 Back in the mid-80s, the National Science Foundation decided to establish supercomputing

2
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:16,520
 centers and the University of Michigan was one of the organizations that made a proposal

3
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:19,000
 for a national supercomputing center.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:28,760
 The Michigan proposal had as its primary hardware artifact, a machine that was built in Japan.

5
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:34,400
 And I explained to my colleagues, my new colleagues, the University of Michigan having just arrived

6
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:38,200
 that it was highly unlikely that the proposal would be funded.

7
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:44,120
 A short while later, I was visiting the National Science Foundation and I had gotten to know,

8
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:48,040
 not well, but had gotten to know Eric Block, who was then director of the National Science

9
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:51,040
 Foundation.

10
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:57,760
 And Eric and I had a conversation about Michigan's proposal.

11
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:04,760
 And it was clear to me from my conversation with Eric that there was no prospect that

12
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:07,160
 the Michigan proposal would be funded.

13
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:12,640
 I said to Eric, I said, "Well, it occurs to me that what might be even better for the

14
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:18,520
 University of Michigan than having a supercomputing center is to run the network that connects

15
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:19,520
 all of the supercomputing centers."

16
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:28,120
 At any rate, I had an old friend who worked for IBM Research by the name of Al Weiss,

17
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:32,600
 who's in charge of all of IBM Research's computing facilities.

18
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:41,560
 And I called Al and I said, "Al, this is a great opportunity, but IBM is not going

19
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:45,920
 to be successful here and I need your help."

20
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:50,000
 And so Al sort of rallied some folks from IBM Research, where there actually was work

21
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,320
 going on in TCP/IP protocols.

22
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:59,120
 We got tentatively an agreement from IBM that they would contribute the hardware and the

23
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:04,600
 software to create the routing structure for the network.

24
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:10,520
 But we still needed the communications facilities.

25
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:21,240
 And at that time, the CFO at IBM was a gentleman, I think I remember the name correctly, his

26
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:23,840
 last name was Crowe.

27
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:33,200
 And so through IBM, we went to him and he had contacts with a former IBM employee who

28
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:45,520
 was now the Chief Technical Officer and essentially the Chief Network Operations Officer for MCI.

29
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:49,080
 His name was Dick Liebhaber.

30
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:56,040
 And so IBM approached Dick Liebhaber and asked him if MCI would be interested in providing

31
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:59,000
 the communications facilities for this network.

32
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:05,200
 Well, as you may recall, at that time, MCI was this fledgling organization.

33
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:12,800
 Some people had described it really as a law office trying to create an environment where

34
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:18,200
 they could actually offer telecommunication services up against AT&T's lobbying efforts.

35
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:19,800
 They had just been successful in that.

36
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:26,640
 They were establishing facilities across the United States.

37
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:34,600
 And Dick Liebhaber saw this as an opportunity to sort of move MCI into the big time to be

38
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:37,480
 part of this NSFNET proposal.

39
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:45,000
 And so we wound up with an agreement that we would file a joint proposal with Merit

40
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:53,480
 as the principal organization in partnership with IBM who would build all the routing hardware

41
00:03:53,480 --> 00:04:00,120
 and software and with MCI who would provide the nationwide communications facilities.

42
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:07,240
 And while we were--and then we got Governor Blanchard to commit a million dollars a year

43
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:10,440
 from state funds in addition.

44
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:15,240
 So we wound up being able to submit a proposal to the National Science Foundation I think

45
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:21,160
 for something like $14.7 million because we knew the ceiling was 15.

46
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:29,520
 But in fact, by including all this in kind activity, it was actually more like a $55

47
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:31,280
 million proposal.

48
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:42,600
 It was designed to start at T1 or 1.5 megabits with planned upgrades over the period of the

49
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:46,040
 network's life.

50
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:52,520
 We subsequently learned that the proposal was received with considerable skepticism

51
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:56,440
 by the reviewers of the National Science Foundation.

52
00:04:56,440 --> 00:05:00,840
 People really wondered about our technical ability to pull this off.

53
00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:08,840
 But that review was conducted without reference to the actual funding pattern.

54
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:15,860
 And then when the wraps got pulled off of the amount of resource that was being committed

55
00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:22,400
 by the partners to this proposal, it immediately went to the top of the list at the NSF.

56
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:28,640
 And a short period later, we received informal word that they wanted to negotiate with us

57
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:31,280
 about sort of working this all out.

58
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,600
 But we had to do a lot of innovation.

59
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:38,520
 The border gateway protocols had to be developed to allow multiple networks to interact with

60
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:41,800
 one another.

61
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:49,760
 And we had to build increasingly more capable routing and communications facilities.

62
00:05:49,760 --> 00:06:02,520
 When we started the network, we had T1 circuits, but there were no cards for computers that

63
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:05,240
 would go at 1.5 megabits.

64
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:11,400
 We used the T1 circuits we subdivided and built a mesh network among all of the routers

65
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:14,160
 that we put in place.

66
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:20,120
 It wasn't for about a year, IBM was actually to build prototype cards that would go at

67
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:23,120
 1.5 megabits.

68
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:29,800
 When we put those cards in our test network, we discovered they worked just fine.

69
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:34,040
 We put them in the production network, the network started failing on us.

70
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:45,380
 And we discovered after a very tough period that the folks who had built the T1 hardware

71
00:06:45,380 --> 00:06:52,520
 for MCI had planned on using certain bit patterns to do diagnosis on the network and had never

72
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:58,160
 anticipated the notion that anybody would ever use a full 1.5 megabits as a single channel.

73
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:03,920
 They had always thought that it would be broken up into a set of voice circuits at 64 kilobits

74
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:04,920
 each.

75
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:07,920
 And so they didn't have any worry about these patterns ever appearing on their network.

76
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:16,240
 Well, it turned out that that happened with some frequency on the NSF net and it took

77
00:07:16,240 --> 00:07:18,320
 the communications line down when it did.

78
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:24,600
 So they had to re-engineer their hardware to undertake this.

79
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:29,400
 We actually had for a short period of time put some translation circuits in our routers

80
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:33,780
 that actually looked for these bit patterns, took them out and replaced them with something

81
00:07:33,780 --> 00:07:40,540
 else and then reinserted them at the other end to make it work over these T1 circuits.

82
00:07:40,540 --> 00:07:44,720
 So we had a lot of adventure.

83
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:55,640
 Finally in, I think it was around 1990, this network was growing so fast that it was clear

84
00:07:55,640 --> 00:08:01,920
 that these T1 circuits were not going to enable what we needed to do.

85
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:09,920
 So we had to go to the next step which was DS3 or from 1.5 megabits to 45 megabits which

86
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:14,560
 was a very large step, a 30-fold increase in capacity.

87
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:21,360
 And in order to do that we wound up creating another not-for-profit organization called

88
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:23,600
 Advanced Network and Services.

89
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:29,720
 Merit was still the principal investigator on the grant but it subcontracted the development

90
00:08:29,720 --> 00:08:37,160
 of this new network, this 45 megabit network to Advanced Network and Services which was

91
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:41,320
 headquartered in Armonk.

92
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:58,280
 And IBM, MCI and Nortel all contributed $3 million to the founding of this new organization

93
00:08:58,280 --> 00:09:06,920
 so it had the staff and the facilities to do the innovation that required us to go up

94
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:10,400
 to 45 megabits.

95
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:18,240
 That did accommodate our capacity needs over the life of the NSFnet.

96
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:27,840
 The NSFnet was the fastest internet network to the end.

97
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:41,140
 It finally was decommissioned in 1995 when the Congress decided that the federal government

98
00:09:41,140 --> 00:09:48,040
 should not be in the business of supporting something that by that time in their view

99
00:09:48,040 --> 00:09:52,080
 should have become a commercial facility.

100
00:09:52,080 --> 00:10:07,720
 I'll not ever forget sitting in a house hearing room in the Capitol and next to Mitch

101
00:10:07,720 --> 00:10:25,840
 Kapoor and some small internet company startup CEOs who were complaining to the Congress

102
00:10:25,840 --> 00:10:35,200
 that it was inappropriate for the NSFnet to be funded by the National Science Foundation

103
00:10:35,200 --> 00:10:39,760
 when they could provide this service as a commercial service.

104
00:10:39,760 --> 00:10:47,720
 At the very same time they were making that complaint, they were using NSFnet as their

105
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:55,600
 backup network to carry traffic when their much less reliable networks failed on a national

106
00:10:55,600 --> 00:10:56,600
 scale.

107
00:10:56,600 --> 00:11:04,760
 MCI of course turned out to be a major internet service provider also using the same technology.

108
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:15,720
 In a classic innovator's curse moment, IBM who was at that time the leader in routing

109
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:28,120
 technology for internet backbones, managed to decide that they should kill all of the

110
00:11:28,120 --> 00:11:33,600
 work they had done in developing these routers because it would threaten their proprietary

111
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:35,160
 network efforts.

112
00:11:35,160 --> 00:11:40,320
 It's probably almost single handedly responsible for the fact that Cisco became the dominant

113
00:11:40,320 --> 00:11:44,160
 router company in the United States rather than IBM.

